{{>header}}

<article class="doc-page">
  <header class="page-intro">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="page-intro__eyebrow">Platforms</p>
      <h1 class="page-intro__title">Supported platforms</h1>
      <p class="page-intro__lede">
        Each platform maps ActivityStreams 2.0 onto a specific network. Same
        JSON envelope on the way in, same shape on the way back. If you need
        more, the platform interface is small enough to implement in an
        afternoon.
      </p>
      <nav class="platforms-toc" aria-label="Platform sections">
        <a href="#irc">IRC</a>
        <a href="#xmpp">XMPP</a>
        <a href="#email">Email</a>
        <a href="#feeds">RSS / Atom</a>
        <a href="#metadata">Metadata</a>
        <a href="#caldav">CalDAV</a>
        <a href="#carddav">CardDAV</a>
        <a href="#messenger">Messenger</a>
        <a href="#instagram">Instagram</a>
        <a href="#whatsapp">WhatsApp</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- ============ IRC ============ -->
  <section class="protocol" id="irc">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="protocol__head">
        <div class="protocol__glyph" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none">
            <circle cx="20" cy="18" r="14" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="20" y="25" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="currentColor">#</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="protocol__name">IRC</h2>
          <p class="protocol__sub">Chat and channels on the original open protocol.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="protocol__link" href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@sockethub/platform-irc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          @sockethub/platform-irc ↗
        </a>
      </header>

      <div class="protocol__body">
        <p>
          Speak to any IRC network — Libera, OFTC, your local. Sockethub
          handles reconnects, PINGs, and nick collision for you.
        </p>

        <p class="protocol__flow-note">
          The full IRC flow is
          <strong>credentials → connect → join → send</strong>.
          Each envelope shares the same <code>@context</code>; only
          <code>type</code> and the target change.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">1. Credentials</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [
    <span class="tok-str">"https://www.w3.org/ns/activitystreams"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/v1.jsonld"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/platform/irc/v1.jsonld"</span>
  ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"credentials"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  {
    <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"alice@irc.libera.chat"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"name"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"alice"</span>
  },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"credentials"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"nick"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"alice"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"server"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"irc.libera.chat"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"port"</span>:   6697,
    <span class="tok-key">"secure"</span>: true,
    <span class="tok-key">"token"</span>:  <span class="tok-str">"…"</span>
  }
}</code></pre>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">2. Connect</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"connect"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"alice@irc.libera.chat"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"name"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"alice"</span> }
}</code></pre>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">3. Join a channel</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"join"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"alice@irc.libera.chat"</span>,     <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"name"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"alice"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"target"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"#sockethub@irc.libera.chat"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"room"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"name"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"#sockethub"</span> }
}</code></pre>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">4. Send a message</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"send"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"alice@irc.libera.chat"</span>,     <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"name"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"alice"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"target"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"#sockethub@irc.libera.chat"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"room"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"name"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"#sockethub"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"message"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"content"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"hey everyone"</span> }
}</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ XMPP ============ -->
  <section class="protocol" id="xmpp">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="protocol__head">
        <div class="protocol__glyph" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none">
            <path d="M8 10 H32 V26 H24 L20 32 L16 26 H8 Z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
            <circle cx="14" cy="18" r="1.6" fill="currentColor"/>
            <circle cx="20" cy="18" r="1.6" fill="currentColor"/>
            <circle cx="26" cy="18" r="1.6" fill="currentColor"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="protocol__name">XMPP</h2>
          <p class="protocol__sub">Federated messaging, rooms, and presence.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="protocol__link" href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@sockethub/platform-xmpp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          @sockethub/platform-xmpp ↗
        </a>
      </header>

      <div class="protocol__body">
        <p>
          Connect to any XMPP service — public or self-hosted. Multi-user
          chat, direct messages, presence, and roster updates all come
          through as ActivityStreams objects.
        </p>

        <p class="protocol__flow-note">
          Full XMPP MUC flow is
          <strong>credentials → connect → join → send</strong>. For a 1:1
          message to a contact, skip <code>join</code> and target the
          person JID directly (shown at the end).
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">1. Credentials</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [
    <span class="tok-str">"https://www.w3.org/ns/activitystreams"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/v1.jsonld"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/platform/xmpp/v1.jsonld"</span>
  ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"credentials"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  {
    <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"alice@chat.example"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span>
  },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:        <span class="tok-str">"credentials"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"userAddress"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"alice@chat.example"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"password"</span>:    <span class="tok-str">"…"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"server"</span>:      <span class="tok-str">"chat.example"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"resource"</span>:    <span class="tok-str">"sockethub"</span>
  }
}</code></pre>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">2. Connect</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"connect"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"alice@chat.example"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> }
}</code></pre>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">3. Join a room (MUC)</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"join"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"alice@chat.example"</span>,              <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"target"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"sockethub@conference.chat.example"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"room"</span> }
}</code></pre>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">4a. Send to the room</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"send"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"alice@chat.example"</span>,              <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"target"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"sockethub@conference.chat.example"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"room"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"message"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"content"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"hey everyone"</span> }
}</code></pre>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">4b. Send 1:1 to a contact (no join needed)</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"send"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"alice@chat.example"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"target"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"bob@chat.example"</span>,   <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"message"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"content"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"ping"</span> }
}</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Email ============ -->
  <section class="protocol" id="email">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="protocol__head">
        <div class="protocol__glyph" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none">
            <rect x="7" y="11" width="26" height="18" rx="3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
            <path d="M7 14 L20 23 L33 14" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="protocol__name">Email</h2>
          <p class="protocol__sub">Receive over IMAP, send over SMTP.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="protocol__link" href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@sockethub/platform-email" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          @sockethub/platform-email ↗
        </a>
        <span class="protocol__badge">New in 5.0</span>
      </header>

      <div class="protocol__body">
        <p>
          Read a mailbox and send mail from the browser. The mail server
          stays the message store: Sockethub keeps no mailbox database and
          never changes read flags, moves messages, or deletes mail. Each
          client session gets its own IMAP connection, so two clients on the
          same account can watch different folders.
        </p>

        <p class="protocol__flow-note">
          The full email flow is
          <strong>credentials → connect → fetch / watch / send</strong>.
          Configure IMAP, SMTP, or both; each needs a username and exactly
          one of <code>password</code> or <code>token</code>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">1. Credentials</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [
    <span class="tok-str">"https://www.w3.org/ns/activitystreams"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/v1.jsonld"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/platform/email/v1.jsonld"</span>
  ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"credentials"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"me@example.com"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"name"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"Alice Example"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"credentials"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"from"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"me@example.com"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"imap"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"host"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"imap.example.com"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"port"</span>: 993, <span class="tok-key">"secure"</span>: true,
              <span class="tok-key">"username"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"me@example.com"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"password"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"…"</span> },
    <span class="tok-key">"smtp"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"host"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"smtp.example.com"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"port"</span>: 587, <span class="tok-key">"secure"</span>: false,
              <span class="tok-key">"username"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"me@example.com"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"token"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"…"</span> }
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          <code>secure: true</code> uses TLS immediately; <code>false</code>
          requires STARTTLS. Plaintext credentials are never accepted.
          <code>token</code> is an access token your app obtained itself;
          Sockethub runs no OAuth flow and does not refresh tokens.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">2. Connect</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:  <span class="tok-str">"connect"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"me@example.com"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> }
}</code></pre>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">3. Fetch a page of messages</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"fetch"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"me@example.com"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"folders"</span>: [ <span class="tok-str">"INBOX"</span> ],
    <span class="tok-key">"from"</span>:    [ <span class="tok-str">"friend@example.com"</span> ],
    <span class="tok-key">"limit"</span>:   20
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          The reply is a <code>collection</code> of <code>post</code>
          activities (sender as actor, a <code>message</code> as object) plus
          per-folder <code>cursors</code> and <code>hasMore</code>. Send the
          cursors back in the next <code>fetch</code> to get the next page.
          Attachments are listed by filename and type; their contents are
          never returned.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">4. Watch a folder for new mail</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"watch"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"me@example.com"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"folder"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"INBOX"</span> }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          New messages arrive on your <code>message</code> listener as
          <code>post</code> activities, using IMAP IDLE where the server
          supports it. One watch per session; <code>unwatch</code> stops it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">5. Send</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"send"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"me@example.com"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"name"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"Alice Example"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"to"</span>:      [ <span class="tok-str">"friend@example.com"</span> ],
    <span class="tok-key">"subject"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"Hello"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"content"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"Hello from Sockethub!"</span>
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          The acknowledgement carries a <code>submission</code> with the
          server's <code>messageId</code> and the <code>accepted</code> and
          <code>rejected</code> recipients. Acceptance means the SMTP server
          took the message, not that it was delivered. No Sent-folder copy is
          saved.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Feeds ============ -->
  <section class="protocol" id="feeds">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="protocol__head">
        <div class="protocol__glyph" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none">
            <path d="M8 14 a18 18 0 0 1 18 18" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"/>
            <path d="M8 8 a24 24 0 0 1 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"/>
            <circle cx="9" cy="31" r="2.2" fill="currentColor"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="protocol__name">RSS / Atom</h2>
          <p class="protocol__sub">Fetch, parse, and stream updates from feeds.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="protocol__link" href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@sockethub/platform-feeds" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          @sockethub/platform-feeds ↗
        </a>
      </header>

      <div class="protocol__body">
        <p>
          Point the feeds platform at any RSS or Atom URL and it returns
          normalized entries as ActivityStreams objects. Useful for reader
          apps, dashboards, or anywhere you'd otherwise reach for a polling
          cron.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">Fetch once</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [
    <span class="tok-str">"https://www.w3.org/ns/activitystreams"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/v1.jsonld"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/platform/feeds/v1.jsonld"</span>
  ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:  <span class="tok-str">"fetch"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"https://blog.example/feed.xml"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"feed"</span> }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          The feed URL is the <code>actor</code>; there is no target. The
          reply is a <code>collection</code> whose <code>items</code> are
          <code>post</code> activities, one per entry. Add an
          <code>object</code> with <code>since</code> (RFC 3339) and
          <code>limit</code> to fetch only what's new:
        </p>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"fetch"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"https://blog.example/feed.xml"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"feed"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"since"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"2026-11-01T00:00:00Z"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"limit"</span>: 10 }
}</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Metadata ============ -->
  <section class="protocol" id="metadata">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="protocol__head">
        <div class="protocol__glyph" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none">
            <path d="M10 22 l12 -12 h10 v10 l-12 12 z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
            <circle cx="26" cy="14" r="2" fill="currentColor"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="protocol__name">Metadata</h2>
          <p class="protocol__sub">Link previews and structured-data extraction.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="protocol__link" href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@sockethub/platform-metadata" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          @sockethub/platform-metadata ↗
        </a>
        <span class="protocol__badge">New in 5.0</span>
      </header>

      <div class="protocol__body">
        <p>
          Fetch a URL, extract Open Graph tags, the canonical URL, favicon,
          images, and any schema.org <code>Event</code> published as JSON-LD,
          and hand back a single normalized <code>page</code> object. Built
          for chat link previews, reader summaries, and event cards.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">Fetch metadata for a URL</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [
    <span class="tok-str">"https://www.w3.org/ns/activitystreams"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/v1.jsonld"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/platform/metadata/v1.jsonld"</span>
  ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:  <span class="tok-str">"fetch"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"https://blog.example/article"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"website"</span> }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          The page URL is the <code>actor</code>; there is no target. The
          reply's <code>object</code> carries <code>title</code>,
          <code>description</code>, <code>image</code> (an array, possibly
          empty), <code>url</code>, <code>favicon</code>, and — when the page
          declares one — an <code>event</code> with its name, start, end,
          location, and status.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CalDAV ============ -->
  <section class="protocol" id="caldav">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="protocol__head">
        <div class="protocol__glyph" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none">
            <rect x="8" y="10" width="24" height="22" rx="3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
            <path d="M8 17 H32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
            <path d="M14 6 V12 M26 6 V12" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"/>
            <path d="M15 24 l3.5 3.5 L25 21" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="protocol__name">CalDAV</h2>
          <p class="protocol__sub">Calendars, events, and tasks on any CalDAV server.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="protocol__link" href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@sockethub/platform-caldav" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          @sockethub/platform-caldav ↗
        </a>
        <span class="protocol__badge">New in 5.0</span>
      </header>

      <div class="protocol__body">
        <p>
          Discover calendars, then read, create, update, and delete events
          (<code>VEVENT</code>) and tasks (<code>VTODO</code>) on any CalDAV
          server — Nextcloud, Radicale, Fastmail, and friends. Every write is
          guarded by an ETag check, so you never silently overwrite a change
          made by another client.
        </p>

        <p class="protocol__flow-note">
          The full CalDAV flow is
          <strong>credentials → fetch → query / create / update / delete</strong>.
          <code>fetch</code> discovers your calendars; every later request
          targets one of the discovered calendar URLs.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">1. Credentials</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [
    <span class="tok-str">"https://www.w3.org/ns/activitystreams"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/v1.jsonld"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/platform/caldav/v1.jsonld"</span>
  ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"credentials"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  {
    <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"caldav:alice"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span>
  },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:  <span class="tok-str">"credentials"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"url"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"https://calendar.example/dav/"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"token"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"…"</span>
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          <code>token</code> is sent as an HTTP Bearer token (your app runs the
          OAuth flow itself). For Basic auth — typically an app password —
          replace it with <code>username</code> and <code>password</code>. The
          URL can be a service root; discovery also tries
          <code>/.well-known/caldav</code>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">2. Discover calendars</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"fetch"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"caldav:alice"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          The response lists your calendars as objects with an <code>id</code>
          (the calendar URL), a <code>name</code>, a <code>color</code>, and
          which <code>components</code> they support (<code>event</code>,
          <code>task</code>).
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">3. Query events in a date range</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"query"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"caldav:alice"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"target"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"https://calendar.example/calendars/alice/personal/"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"calendar"</span>
  },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:      <span class="tok-str">"event"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"startTime"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"2026-08-01T00:00:00Z"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"endTime"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"2026-09-01T00:00:00Z"</span>
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          Each returned item carries its resource <code>id</code>,
          <code>uid</code>, and <code>etag</code> — keep the ETag for later
          update or delete requests.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">4. Create an event</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"create"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"caldav:alice"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"target"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"https://calendar.example/calendars/alice/personal/"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"calendar"</span>
  },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:      <span class="tok-str">"event"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"name"</span>:      <span class="tok-str">"Project planning"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"startTime"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"2026-08-03T15:00:00"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"endTime"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"2026-08-03T16:00:00"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"timeZone"</span>:  <span class="tok-str">"Europe/Prague"</span>
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          Events and tasks support recurrence, attendees, reminders, and
          attachments. <code>update</code> and <code>delete</code> send the
          item's <code>etag</code>; if another client changed it in the
          meantime you get <code>caldav:conflict</code> back — query again and
          retry with the fresh ETag.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CardDAV ============ -->
  <section class="protocol" id="carddav">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="protocol__head">
        <div class="protocol__glyph" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none">
            <rect x="9" y="8" width="22" height="24" rx="3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
            <path d="M6 14 H9 M6 20 H9 M6 26 H9" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"/>
            <circle cx="20" cy="17" r="3.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
            <path d="M14 27 c0 -3.5 2.7 -5.5 6 -5.5 s6 2 6 5.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="protocol__name">CardDAV</h2>
          <p class="protocol__sub">Contacts and address books on any CardDAV server.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="protocol__link" href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@sockethub/platform-carddav" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          @sockethub/platform-carddav ↗
        </a>
        <span class="protocol__badge">New in 5.0</span>
      </header>

      <div class="protocol__body">
        <p>
          Discover address books, then search, create, update, and delete
          vCard contacts on any CardDAV server — Nextcloud, Radicale,
          Fastmail, and friends. Contacts come back as plain ActivityStreams
          <code>person</code> objects, and every write is guarded by an ETag
          check, so you never silently overwrite a change made by another
          client.
        </p>

        <p class="protocol__flow-note">
          The full CardDAV flow is
          <strong>credentials → fetch → query / create / update / delete</strong>.
          <code>fetch</code> discovers your address books; every later request
          targets one of the discovered address book URLs.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">1. Credentials</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [
    <span class="tok-str">"https://www.w3.org/ns/activitystreams"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/v1.jsonld"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/platform/carddav/v1.jsonld"</span>
  ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"credentials"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  {
    <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"carddav:alice"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span>
  },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:     <span class="tok-str">"credentials"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"url"</span>:      <span class="tok-str">"https://contacts.example/dav/"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"username"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"alice"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"password"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"…"</span>
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          <code>username</code> and <code>password</code> — typically an app
          password — are sent as Basic or Digest auth. For Bearer auth (your
          app runs the OAuth flow itself) replace them with a single
          <code>token</code>. The URL can be a service root; discovery also
          tries <code>/.well-known/carddav</code>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">2. Discover address books</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"fetch"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"carddav:alice"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          The response lists your address books as objects with an
          <code>id</code> (the address book URL) and a <code>name</code>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">3. Search contacts</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"query"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"carddav:alice"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"target"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"https://contacts.example/addressbooks/alice/personal/"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"addressBook"</span>
  },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"contactQuery"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"text"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"Bob"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"fields"</span>: [ <span class="tok-str">"name"</span>, <span class="tok-str">"email"</span> ],
    <span class="tok-key">"limit"</span>:  <span class="tok-str">50</span>
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          Leave <code>text</code> out to list the whole address book. Each
          returned contact is a <code>person</code> with its resource
          <code>id</code>, <code>uid</code>, and <code>etag</code>, plus
          structured <code>emails</code>, <code>telephones</code>,
          <code>addresses</code>, and <code>photoUrls</code> — keep the ETag
          for later update or delete requests.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">4. Create a contact</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"create"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"carddav:alice"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"target"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"https://contacts.example/addressbooks/alice/personal/"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"addressBook"</span>
  },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:       <span class="tok-str">"person"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"name"</span>:       <span class="tok-str">"Bob Example"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"givenName"</span>:  <span class="tok-str">"Bob"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"familyName"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"Example"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"emails"</span>: [
      { <span class="tok-key">"value"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"bob@example.com"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"types"</span>: [ <span class="tok-str">"work"</span> ], <span class="tok-key">"preferred"</span>: <span class="tok-str">true</span> }
    ]
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          The platform reads vCard 3.0 and 4.0 and writes vCard 4.0.
          <code>update</code> sends the complete contact back with its
          <code>etag</code>; properties your app doesn't understand are
          preserved rather than erased. If another client changed the card in
          the meantime you get <code>carddav:conflict</code> back — query again
          and retry with the fresh ETag.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Messenger ============ -->
  <section class="protocol" id="messenger">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="protocol__head">
        <div class="protocol__glyph" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none">
            <path d="M20 7 C12 7 6 12.5 6 19.5 C6 23.5 8 27 11 29.2 L11 34 L16 31.2 C17.3 31.6 18.6 31.8 20 31.8 C28 31.8 34 26.3 34 19.5 C34 12.5 28 7 20 7 Z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
            <path d="M12 21 L17.5 15.5 L21.5 19.5 L28 13.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="protocol__name">Messenger</h2>
          <p class="protocol__sub">Outgoing text messages as a Facebook Page.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="protocol__link" href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@sockethub/platform-messenger" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          @sockethub/platform-messenger ↗
        </a>
        <span class="protocol__badge">New in 5.0</span>
      </header>

      <div class="protocol__body">
        <p>
          Reply to people who message your Facebook Page, through Meta's
          Messenger API. Sockethub sends as the Page, not as a personal
          profile. Incoming messages and recipient discovery stay with your
          own webhook integration; this platform covers the reply.
        </p>

        <p class="protocol__flow-note">
          The full Messenger flow is
          <strong>credentials → send</strong>. The platform is stateless:
          there is no <code>connect</code> step and no webhook to host.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">1. Credentials</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [
    <span class="tok-str">"https://www.w3.org/ns/activitystreams"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/v1.jsonld"</span>,
    <span class="tok-str">"https://sockethub.org/ns/context/platform/messenger/v1.jsonld"</span>
  ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"credentials"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"&lt;account id&gt;"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: {
    <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:       <span class="tok-str">"credentials"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"token"</span>:      <span class="tok-str">"…"</span>,
    <span class="tok-key">"apiVersion"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"v22.0"</span>
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          <code>actor.id</code> is your Facebook Page ID and <code>token</code>
          is a Page access token with <code>pages_messaging</code>, obtained
          outside Sockethub. Choose a Graph API version your Meta app supports;
          it is never guessed.
        </p>

        <h3 class="protocol__section">2. Send a text message</h3>
        <pre class="envelope-block"><code>{
  <span class="tok-key">"@context"</span>: [ <span class="tok-comment">/* as above */</span> ],
  <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>:   <span class="tok-str">"send"</span>,
  <span class="tok-key">"actor"</span>:  { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"&lt;account id&gt;"</span>,   <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"target"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"id"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"&lt;recipient id&gt;"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"person"</span> },
  <span class="tok-key">"object"</span>: { <span class="tok-key">"type"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"message"</span>, <span class="tok-key">"content"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"Hello from Sockethub!"</span> }
}</code></pre>
        <p>
          <code>target.id</code> is the recipient's Page-scoped ID (PSID) from
          your incoming-message integration. Text only, up to 2000 characters.
          The acknowledgement returns your activity with <code>object.id</code>
          set to Meta's message ID, which confirms acceptance by the API, not
          delivery. A closed messaging window comes back as the stable error
          marker <code>meta:messaging-window-closed</code>. Set
          <code>object.humanAgent: true</code> only for a manual support reply
          when your app has Human Agent access.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

